      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The concept of a \"Great Khan\" does not exist in modern geopolitical history, as such a title predates the current territorial disputes in the South China Sea and belongs to historical empires that ceased to operate in this manner centuries ago. Modern states in this region typically rely on a complex mix of verified UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) interpretations, multilateral diplomatic dialogues, and careful calibration of naval transit rights. Escalation is primarily avoided through open communication channels and exploratory sanctions or military demonstrations that signal resolve without crossing the threshold into kinetic force.",
